        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "0494d5ee-57b3-5103-901a-929cddd96207",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Cases: Griswold v. Connecticut; Diamond v. Chakrabarty\n- People: C. Lee Buxton; Ananda Mohan Chakrabarty\n- Organizations: Supreme Court of the United States; Yale School of Medicine; GE\n- Concepts: Right to privacy; Patents for genetically modified organisms; Directed evolution\n\nFacts:\n- In 1965, Griswold v. Connecticut, 381 U.S. 479, became a landmark United States Supreme Court case holding that the Constitution, through the Bill of Rights, implies a fundamental right to privacy.\n- C. Lee Buxton was an American gynecologist, a professor at the Yale School of Medicine, and an appellant in Griswold v. Connecticut.\n- In 1980, Diamond v. Chakrabarty, 447 U.S. 303, was a United States Supreme Court case about whether genetically modified organisms can be patented.\n- Ananda Mohan Chakrabarty is a Bengali American microbiologist, scientist, and researcher known for work in directed evolution and for helping develop a genetically engineered organism using plasmid transfer while working at GE.\n- The patent for the genetically engineered organism developed by Ananda Mohan Chakrabarty led to the landmark Supreme Court case Diamond v. Chakrabarty.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "39e06a83-8186-5994-b26a-a80e33ad286a",
        "text": "Page: Griswold v. Connecticut. Sentence: Griswold v. Connecticut, 381 U.S. 479 (1965) , is a landmark case in the United States in which the Supreme Court of the United States ruled that the Constitution, through the Bill of Rights, implies a fundamental right to privacy.\nPage: Diamond v. Chakrabarty. Sentence: Diamond v. Chakrabarty, 447 U.S. 303 (1980), was a United States Supreme Court case dealing with whether genetically modified organisms can be patented.\nPage: C. Lee Buxton. Sentence: Charles Lee Buxton (October 14, 1904 – July 7, 1969) was an American gynecologist, professor at the Yale School of Medicine, and appellant in US Supreme Court case \"Griswold v. Connecticut\".\nPage: Ananda Mohan Chakrabarty. Sentence: Ananda Mohan Chakrabarty (Bengali: আনন্দমোহন চক্রবর্তী \"Ānandamōhan Cakrabartī\"), Ph.D. is an Bengali American microbiologist, scientist, and researcher, most notable for his work in directed evolution and his role in developing a genetically engineered organism using plasmid transfer while working at GE, the patent for which led to landmark Supreme Court case, \"Diamond v. Chakrabarty\".",
